(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-1-intro-anatomy-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology — Anatomy Exam 1 — Set 2","category":"Anatomy","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"In which planes do flexion and extension usually occur?","choices":["Only the transverse plane","Midsagittal or parasagittal planes","Only the midcoronal plane","No specific plane at all"],"answer":1,"exp":"Flexion and extension usually occur in the midsagittal or parasagittal planes. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What is the difference between flexion and extension?","choices":["Flexion and extension both describe rotation about a vertical axis","Flexion moves away from the ventral surface; extension brings ventral surfaces together","Flexion brings ventral surfaces together; extension moves away from the ventral surface","Both describe the same movement at different joints"],"answer":2,"exp":"Flexion brings primitively ventral surfaces together (e.g., bending the arm at the elbow), while extension is movement away from the ventral surface (e.g., straightening the leg at the knee joint). — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What is plantar flexion versus dorsiflexion of the foot?","choices":["Plantar flexion occurs at the knee joint rather than at the ankle joint itself","Plantar flexion is described as upward flexion of the foot; dorsiflexion is downward flexion of the foot","Both terms are described as referring to the exact same movement occurring at the ankle joint","Plantar flexion is downward flexion (true flexion) at the ankle; dorsiflexion is upward flexion (extension) at the ankle"],"answer":3,"exp":"Plantar flexion is downward flexion (true flexion) of the foot at the ankle joint, while dorsiflexion is upward flexion (extension) of the foot at the ankle joint. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"In which plane do abduction and adduction usually occur?","choices":["No specific plane","The transverse plane","The midsagittal plane","The midcoronal plane"],"answer":3,"exp":"Abduction and adduction usually occur in the midcoronal plane. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What is the difference between abduction and adduction?","choices":["Abduction only applies to the wrist; adduction only applies to the ankle","Abduction is movement toward the median; adduction is movement away from the median","Abduction is movement away from the median; adduction is movement toward the median","Both describe rotation about a vertical axis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Abduction (lateral flexion) is movement away from the median (away from the middle finger/2nd toe), while adduction is movement toward the median (toward the middle finger/2nd toe). — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What are radial deviation and ulnar deviation, as specific examples of abduction/adduction at the wrist?","choices":["Radial deviation is abduction of the hand at the wrist; ulnar deviation is adduction of the hand at the wrist","Both radial and ulnar deviation occur at the elbow joint rather than at the wrist","Radial deviation is adduction of the hand; ulnar deviation is abduction of the hand","Both terms describe the exact same movement occurring at the wrist"],"answer":0,"exp":"Radial deviation is abduction of the hand at the wrist joint, and ulnar deviation is adduction of the hand at the wrist joint. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"About what axis do medial and lateral rotation usually occur?","choices":["No fixed axis","A horizontal axis in the coronal plane","The axis of the sagittal plane only","A vertical axis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Medial rotation and lateral rotation usually occur about a vertical axis. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What is the difference between medial rotation and lateral rotation?","choices":["Medial and lateral rotation only apply to the ankle","Medial rotation moves the ventral surface away from the median; lateral rotation moves it toward the median","Medial rotation moves the ventral surface toward the median; lateral rotation moves it away from the median","Both describe the same rotational movement"],"answer":2,"exp":"Medial rotation is movement of the ventral surface toward the median (e.g., bringing a flexed arm across the chest), while lateral rotation is movement of the ventral surface away from the median (e.g., directing the head toward one side). — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What is the difference between pronation and supination of the hand?","choices":["Pronation and supination describe movements of the foot, not the hand","Pronation is lateral rotation so the palm faces anteriorly; supination is medial rotation so the palm faces posteriorly","Both describe the same wrist movement","Pronation is medial rotation so the palm faces posteriorly; supination is lateral rotation so the palm faces anteriorly"],"answer":3,"exp":"Pronation is medial rotation so the palm faces posteriorly, while supination is lateral rotation so the palm faces anteriorly (as when holding a cup of soup). — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"What is the difference between inversion and eversion of the foot?","choices":["Both describe the same foot movement","Inversion rotates the plantar surface inward; eversion rotates it laterally","Inversion rotates the plantar surface laterally; eversion rotates it inward","Inversion and eversion describe movements of the hand, not the foot"],"answer":1,"exp":"Inversion rotates the plantar (sole) surface inward, while eversion rotates the plantar surface laterally. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What is circumduction?","choices":["A purely rotational movement occurring solely about a single vertical axis","A movement unique to the eye, termed intorsion and extorsion","Movement of the thumb into an opposed position against the fingers only","A combined movement involving flexion/extension plus abduction/adduction"],"answer":3,"exp":"Circumduction is a combined movement involving two pairs of movement: flexion/extension plus abduction/adduction. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What is opposition/reposition of the thumb described as in the material?","choices":["A specific movement that is described as occurring only at the wrist joint","A movement mechanically identical to circumduction of the joint","A unique human characteristic involving rotation about a resultant axis","A purely rotational movement of the eyeball within its socket only"],"answer":2,"exp":"Opposition/reposition of the thumb is described as a unique human characteristic, involving rotation about a resultant axis. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What are the two sets of internal body cavities described in the material?","choices":["Thoracic cavity and pelvic cavity","Dorsal body cavity and ventral body cavity","Cranial cavity and abdominal cavity","The synovial cavity together with the oral cavity"],"answer":1,"exp":"The two sets of internal body cavities are the dorsal body cavity and the ventral body cavity, both closed to the environment and providing differing degrees of protection to organs. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"What does the dorsal body cavity protect, and what are its two subdivisions?","choices":["Protects the digestive organs; its subdivisions are the thoracic cavity and the pelvic cavity","Protects the nervous system; subdivided into the cranial cavity (encasing the brain) and vertebral cavity (encasing the spinal cord)","Protects the reproductive organs; its subdivisions are the paired left and right pleural cavities","Protects the digestive organs; its subdivisions are the thoracic cavity and the abdominal cavity"],"answer":1,"exp":"The dorsal body cavity protects the nervous system and has two subdivisions: the cranial cavity, which encases the brain, and the vertebral cavity, which encases the spinal cord. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"What does the ventral body cavity house, and what separates its two subdivisions?","choices":["Houses internal organs (viscera); the thoracic and abdominopelvic cavities are separated by the diaphragm","Houses reproductive organs exclusively; its subdivisions are separated by the muscular pelvic floor","Houses only digestive organs; subdivisions separated by the mesentery","Houses the brain and the spinal cord; its subdivisions are separated by the bony vertebral column"],"answer":0,"exp":"The ventral body cavity houses internal organs (viscera) and has two subdivisions — the thoracic cavity and the abdominopelvic cavity — separated by the diaphragm. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"What are the subdivisions of the thoracic cavity, and what does the mediastinum contain?","choices":["Only the pericardial and paired pleural cavities, with no mediastinum described at all","Two pleural cavities (each housing a lung) and the mediastinum, which contains the pericardial cavity, esophagus, trachea, and thymus","The abdominal and pelvic cavities, containing the stomach, intestines, and bladder","Two separate pericardial cavities, one enclosing each lung individually rather than the heart"],"answer":1,"exp":"The thoracic cavity's subdivisions are two pleural cavities (each housing a lung) and the mediastinum, which contains the pericardial cavity, esophagus, trachea, thymus, thoracic duct, and cardiac and phrenic nerves, surrounding the thoracic organs. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What are the subdivisions of the abdominopelvic cavity, and what does each contain?","choices":["Cranial cavity, containing the brain, and vertebral cavity, containing the spinal cord","Pleural cavity, containing the lungs, and pericardial cavity, containing the heart","Thoracic cavity, containing the heart and lungs, and pelvic cavity, containing the bladder","Abdominal cavity (stomach, intestines, spleen, liver) and pelvic cavity (urinary bladder, reproductive organs, rectum)"],"answer":3,"exp":"The abdominopelvic cavity's subdivisions are the abdominal cavity, containing the stomach, intestines, spleen, and liver, and the pelvic cavity, containing the urinary bladder, reproductive organs, and rectum. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"What are the three named serous membranes, and with which cavity/organ is each associated?","choices":["Pericardium (lungs), pleurae (heart), and peritoneum (brain)","Pericardium (heart), pleurae (lungs), and peritoneum (abdominopelvic cavity)","Peritoneum (heart), pleurae (abdominopelvic cavity), and pericardium (lungs)","Meninges (brain), pleurae (lungs), and pericardium (heart)"],"answer":1,"exp":"The three named serous membranes are the pericardium (associated with the heart), the pleurae (associated with the lungs), and the peritoneum (associated with the abdominopelvic cavity). — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"What is the difference between parietal serosa and visceral serosa?","choices":["Both layers are said to specifically cover internal organs only, never the cavity walls","Parietal serosa lines internal body cavity walls; visceral serosa covers internal organs","Both layers are said to specifically line the body cavity walls only, never the organs","Parietal serosa covers organs; visceral serosa lines cavity walls"],"answer":1,"exp":"Parietal serosa lines the internal body cavity walls, while visceral serosa covers the internal organs (viscera); the two layers are separated by a slit-like cavity filled with serous fluid. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What is the function of the serous fluid secreted by the serous membrane layers?","choices":["Filters the blood plasma of waste products","Protects and provides lubrication (antifriction)","Transports nutrients to organs","Produces new immune cells for the body"],"answer":1,"exp":"The serous fluid, secreted by both layers of the serous membrane, protects and provides lubrication (an antifriction function) as organs move against the cavity wall. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"How many abdominopelvic regions are delineated by four planes, per the material?","choices":["Two","Twelve","Four","Nine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Nine abdominopelvic regions are delineated by four planes. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which organ is located in the epigastric region, per the nine-region diagram?","choices":["Stomach","Cecum","Appendix","Urinary bladder"],"answer":0,"exp":"The stomach is shown in the epigastric region in the nine-region diagram. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which organs are located in the right iliac (inguinal) region, per the nine-region diagram?","choices":["Urinary bladder and prostate","Cecum and appendix","Spleen and stomach","Liver and gallbladder"],"answer":1,"exp":"The cecum and appendix are shown in the right iliac (inguinal) region. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which structures are located in the hypogastric (pubic) region, per the nine-region diagram?","choices":["Urinary bladder, prostate, and internal sexual organs","Liver and gallbladder tissue structures","Spleen and diaphragm tissue structures","Ascending colon and cecum tissue"],"answer":0,"exp":"The urinary bladder, prostate, and internal sexual organs are shown in the hypogastric (pubic) region. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which body cavities are exposed to the environment, per the material?","choices":["Cranial and vertebral cavities only","The synovial cavities exclusively","Oral, digestive, and nasal cavities","Pleural and pericardial cavities only"],"answer":2,"exp":"The oral and digestive cavities, and the nasal cavity, are exposed to the environment (along with orbital and middle ear cavities). — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"What are synovial cavities, and are they exposed to the environment?","choices":["Joint fluid-containing cavities, exposed to the environment","Cavities housing the middle ear, not exposed to the environment","Cavities housing the eyes, exposed to the environment","Joint fluid-containing cavities, not exposed to the environment"],"answer":3,"exp":"Synovial cavities contain joint fluid and are listed among the cavities not exposed to the environment. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"What are the six levels of structural organization of the body, from smallest to largest, per the material?","choices":["Tissue, cellular, chemical, organ system, organ, organismal","Organ, tissue, cellular, chemical, organismal, organ system","Chemical, cellular, tissue, organ, organ system, organismal","Cellular, chemical, organ, tissue, organismal, organ system"],"answer":2,"exp":"The levels of structural organization, from smallest to largest, are chemical (atoms/molecules/organelles), cellular (cells), tissue (groups of similar cells), organ (two or more tissue types), organ system (organs working together), and organismal (all organ systems). — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"What defines the \"organ\" level of structural organization?","choices":["Atoms and molecules combined together","Contains two or more types of functional tissues","A single type of similar cells only","All organ systems combined together"],"answer":1,"exp":"An organ contains two or more types of functional tissues. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"What defines the \"organ system\" level of structural organization?","choices":["Atoms combining to form molecules","Organs that work closely together","Groups of similar cells","A single organ performing one function"],"answer":1,"exp":"An organ system consists of organs that work closely together. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"What does the material say about anatomical variation between individuals?","choices":["Variation only occurs in bones, and never occurs in vessels, nerves, or muscles between people","All individuals are said to have completely identical anatomy, with absolutely no variation present anywhere","Structures may vary considerably in detail, even between the right and left sides of the same person, and this must be considered in clinical practice","Variation is only relevant during cadaveric study, never in living clinical practice or diagnosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Structures of individuals, and even the right and left sides of the same person, may vary considerably in detail; bones, muscles, vessels, and nerves may all be unique, and these variations must be considered when performing physical examinations, diagnosis, and treatment. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 44"}]};
